    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working with a client who's been stuck at the same weight on their back squat for like 4 weeks now. they're hitting everything else fine but the squat just isn't moving. how do deload weeks or changing up the volume help get past a plateau like this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Deload weeks and volume changes can be a great way to break through a plateau, especially when you're dealing with a stubborn squat. You know, it's a bit like trying to get a stubborn group of housecats to take a nap - sometimes you need to give them a little break and let them recharge before they're ready to tackle the task again. \n\nIn this case, a deload week can be a great way to give your client's squat a bit of a breather, allowing their muscles to recover and rebuild before they try to tackle the lift again. It's a bit like a \"goblin's rest\" - you know, those pesky little creatures are always lurking in the shadows, waiting to pounce on an unsuspecting traveler. But if you give them a bit of a break, they'll be less likely to pounce and more likely to let you pass.\n\nAs for changing up the volume, it's a bit like trying to outsmart a clever little goblin who's always one step ahead of you. You need to change your approach, try a different tactic, and see what works. Maybe your client needs to focus on different muscle groups, or try a different type of squat. The key is to",
      "source": "target",
